# Rewritten Text

Made from soft, flexible materials such as silicone latex or thermoplastic elastomer, this patient-operable nasal irrigation catheter features a sealed tip at one end and an unobstructed opening at the other. Several perforations are positioned along the sides near the sealed tip. When paired with a connector and attached to a syringe, the device generates numerous forceful narrow jets that clean mucus and dried secretions from the nasal passages and nasopharyngeal region. To enhance maneuverability, the catheter may incorporate one or more internal guide wires (stylets).

When tested at an injection speed of 10 cc per second, the total surface area of the side perforations determines the expected vertical reach of the fluid streams, allowing the catheters to be sorted into three pressure categories:

1. **Low-pressure type**: Side-hole area ranging from 3.367 to 4.123 mm², producing jet heights between 30 and 45 cm;
2. **Medium-pressure type**: Side-hole area ranging from 2.381 to 3.367 mm², producing jet heights between 45 and 90 cm;
3. **High-pressure type**: Side-hole area under 2.381 mm², producing jet heights exceeding 90 cm.
